{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 23, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, when he sins and feels guilt.</b> Rashi explained: when he recognizes within himself to return in repentance, etc. — for every sinner does not ordinarily lay guilt upon himself, and the way of a man seems upright in his own eyes, for he instructs himself that everything is permitted in all his ways, saying that this is owed to him from somewhere else according to his own reasoning, or he imagines that the other did him some wrong, therefore he withholds his deposit. Therefore, on the day that he lays guilt upon himself and recognizes his error and that he did not deal well with his fellow, this is what is meant by ״וְאָשֵׁם״ — that he holds himself guilty; and he, who had been instructing himself that everything was permitted, will now also instruct himself as to the prohibition, through his recognition of his sins, and then he will surely set his heart to return in repentance. ״וְהֵשִׁיב אֶת הַגְּזֵלָה אֲשֶׁר גָּזָל״ — not that he pays the value of the stolen goods, but that very thing which he stole itself. ״וְשִׁלַּם אוֹתוֹ בְּרֹאשׁוֹ״ etc. — for the Holy One, blessed be He, is lenient toward what is His own and does not insist strictly if he delays the time of bringing his guilt-offering to HaShem; therefore He commanded him to give precedence to restitution to a person — to pay him in full, the entire principal, first — and only afterward to bring his guilt-offering for the oath. ״וַחֲמִשִׁיתָיו יוֹסֵף עָלָיו״ — because his money had been sitting idle [deprived] from the other." "And the matter of the fifth is this: because the Holy One, blessed be He, gives a man gold and an abundance of pearls so that he may spend generously and give of his own to the name of HaShem, for the poor of His people — and our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Ketubot 50a): whoever wishes to spend generously, let him not spend more than a fifth, as it is written (Genesis 28:22), ״עַשֵּׂר אֲעַשְּׂרֶנּוּ לָךְ״ — and the second tithe is like the first tithe. Now this man not only failed to spend to give a fifth of his money for the needs of the Sanctuary or for the support of his fellow — but on the contrary, he stole from what belongs to the Sanctuary or from what belongs to his fellow. Therefore justice requires punishing him with the addition of a fifth: for the robbery of the principal is atoned by the restoration of the stolen goods, and what he failed to give as a fifth is atoned for by the fifth he now adds."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:23.055176641Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}